      <article class="article">

        <h2>Why Kiwis Love Brisbane</h2>
        <p>Brisbane is the fastest-growing major city in Australia, and it feels the most like home for many Kiwis. The pace is relaxed, the people are friendly, and the weather is unbeatable — 283 days of sunshine per year. The Gold Coast is just an hour south, and the Sunshine Coast an hour north.</p>
        <p>Brisbane also has the largest Kiwi community per capita in Australia, particularly in Logan and the southern suburbs. You'll hear Kiwi accents at the supermarket. It's the most affordable of the big three cities, and with the 2032 Olympics on the horizon, infrastructure investment is massive — meaning jobs and rising property values.</p>

        <h2>Jobs &amp; Industries in Brisbane</h2>
        <ul>
          <li><strong>Construction &amp; trades</strong> — Biggest boom in the country. Olympics infrastructure, Cross River Rail, Queens Wharf. Electricians and builders earn $80k–$120k with overtime.</li>
          <li><strong>Mining services</strong> — Brisbane is the HQ for Queensland's massive mining industry. FIFO roles pay $100k–$180k.</li>
          <li><strong>Healthcare</strong> — Expanding hospital network. Nurses earn $72k–$90k. Allied health in demand.</li>
          <li><strong>Government</strong> — Queensland state government is the largest employer. Admin, policy, and IT roles.</li>
          <li><strong>Tourism &amp; hospitality</strong> — Gold Coast and Sunshine Coast tourism supports thousands of jobs. Growing cruise ship industry.</li>
          <li><strong>Logistics &amp; transport</strong> — Port of Brisbane and major distribution centres. Forklift, warehouse, and truck driving roles plentiful.</li>
        </ul>

        <h2>Cost of Living in Brisbane</h2>
        <div style="overflow-x:auto;margin:20px 0">
          <table class="data-table">
            <thead>
              <tr>
                <th>Monthly Expense</th>
                <th>Inner City (AUD)</th>
                <th>Suburbs (AUD)</th>
              </tr>
            </thead>
            <tbody>
              <tr><td>2-bed apartment rent</td><td class="price">$2,200–$2,800</td><td class="price">$1,600–$2,200</td></tr>
              <tr><td>Groceries (couple)</td><td>$500–$650</td><td>$450–$600</td></tr>
              <tr><td>Public transport (go card)</td><td>$120–$155</td><td>$140–$180</td></tr>
              <tr><td>Utilities (electricity + water)</td><td>$200–$280</td><td>$180–$260</td></tr>
              <tr><td>Internet (NBN)</td><td>$70–$90</td><td>$70–$90</td></tr>
              <tr><td>Air conditioning (summer)</td><td>$80–$150</td><td>$80–$150</td></tr>
            </tbody>
          </table>
        </div>
        <p>Brisbane is roughly 25–30% cheaper than Sydney and 10–15% cheaper than Melbourne. See our full <a href="/moving-costs">moving costs breakdown</a>.</p>

        <h2>Best Suburbs for Kiwis in Brisbane</h2>

        <div class="checklist-box">
          <h3>Biggest Kiwi communities</h3>
          <ul>
            <li><strong>Logan (Woodridge, Kingston, Marsden)</strong> — The heartland of Kiwis in Brisbane. Largest Pacific Islander community in Australia. Affordable housing, familiar culture, Kiwi shops and churches. 30 min to CBD.</li>
            <li><strong>Ipswich</strong> — West of Brisbane. Rapidly growing, very affordable. Large Māori and Pasifika community. Great for families.</li>
            <li><strong>Inala / Durack</strong> — South-west Brisbane. Multicultural, affordable. Close to the new rail links.</li>
          </ul>
        </div>
        <div class="checklist-box" style="margin-top:16px">
          <h3>Inner city &amp; professionals</h3>
          <ul>
            <li><strong>South Brisbane / West End</strong> — Trendy riverside precinct. Restaurants, markets, galleries. Walking distance to CBD. Popular with young professionals.</li>
            <li><strong>Fortitude Valley / New Farm</strong> — Nightlife, brunch culture, riverside walks. Brisbane's trendiest areas.</li>
            <li><strong>Kangaroo Point / Woolloongabba</strong> — Close to the Gabba (Olympics main stadium). Great river views, easy city access.</li>
          </ul>
        </div>
        <div class="checklist-box" style="margin-top:16px">
          <h3>Family-friendly suburbs</h3>
          <ul>
            <li><strong>Carindale / Mt Gravatt</strong> — Eastern suburbs, good schools, big shopping centres. Middle-class family area.</li>
            <li><strong>Chermside / Aspley</strong> — Northside. Westfield Chermside, hospital nearby. Good transport links.</li>
            <li><strong>Springfield</strong> — Master-planned community south-west. New schools, parks, train station. Very popular with young Kiwi families.</li>
          </ul>
        </div>

        <h2>The Gold Coast — An Hour South</h2>
        <p>Many Kiwis moving to South East Queensland choose the Gold Coast over Brisbane itself. Key areas:</p>
        <ul>
          <li><strong>Southport / Labrador</strong> — central Gold Coast, affordable, close to the tram line.</li>
          <li><strong>Coomera / Pimpama</strong> — northern Gold Coast, new housing estates, family-friendly, affordable. 40 min to Brisbane CBD by train.</li>
          <li><strong>Robina / Varsity Lakes</strong> — university area, good schools, family-friendly.</li>
          <li><strong>Surfers Paradise</strong> — iconic but touristy. Better for hospitality workers than families.</li>
        </ul>

        <h2>Shipping from NZ to Brisbane</h2>
        <div style="overflow-x:auto;margin:20px 0">
          <table class="data-table">
            <thead>
              <tr>
                <th>Move Size</th>
                <th>Estimated Cost (NZD)</th>
                <th>Transit Time</th>
              </tr>
            </thead>
            <tbody>
              <tr><td>Boxes only (1–10)</td><td class="price">$800–$2,000</td><td>2–4 weeks</td></tr>
              <tr><td>1-bed apartment</td><td class="price">$2,500–$4,500</td><td>3–4 weeks</td></tr>
              <tr><td>2–3 bed house</td><td class="price">$5,000–$8,500</td><td>3–4 weeks</td></tr>
              <tr><td>4+ bed house (full container)</td><td class="price">$8,500–$14,000</td><td>3–5 weeks</td></tr>
            </tbody>
          </table>
        </div>
        <p>Auckland to Brisbane is a direct route — one of the fastest trans-Tasman shipping lanes. Read our <a href="/shipping-to-australia-from-nz">complete shipping guide</a>.</p>

        <h2>Weather — Subtropical Paradise</h2>
        <ul>
          <li><strong>Summer (Dec–Feb):</strong> 25–33°C, humid, afternoon storms. Air conditioning is essential.</li>
          <li><strong>Autumn (Mar–May):</strong> 20–28°C, dry, beautiful. Best time of year.</li>
          <li><strong>Winter (Jun–Aug):</strong> 10–22°C, dry, sunny. Mildest winter of any major Australian city.</li>
          <li><strong>Spring (Sep–Nov):</strong> 18–28°C, warming up. Storm season begins in November.</li>
        </ul>
        <p>If you're from Auckland, Brisbane's climate is like a warmer, sunnier version. You'll never own a heavy coat again.</p>

        <h2>First Steps When You Arrive</h2>
        <ol>
          <li><strong>Apply for a TFN</strong> — essential for employment.</li>
          <li><strong>Open a bank account</strong> — CommBank and Suncorp (Queensland's local bank) are popular.</li>
          <li><strong>Enrol in Medicare</strong> — Kiwis are eligible.</li>
          <li><strong>Get a go card</strong> — for buses, trains, and ferries.</li>
          <li><strong>Find a rental</strong> — Domain.com.au and RealEstate.com.au. Brisbane's rental market is tighter than it was but still more options than Sydney.</li>
        </ol>
        <p>Full checklist: <a href="/moving-checklist">Moving to Australia from NZ — step by step</a>.</p>

